<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deccan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Deccan is the large plateau region of peninsular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada River</a>. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>, whose rise and military style were shaped by its hills and forts, and a persistent zone of conflict for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, the difficulty of controlling the Deccan contributed to provincial breakaway and decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deccan.jpg" alt="Deccan" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PadFoot2008 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deccan,_India.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
